-<section class="card"><div class="eyebrow">Section 1</div><h2 class="section-title">How the tables connect</h2><div class="category-list" style="margin-top:14px">
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Account → Allocation</strong><p class="muted">One Account owns two allocation rows: <b>spendable</b> and <b>savings</b>. Allocation.balance is the split of Account.total_balance.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Account → Transaction</strong><p class="muted">Every deposit, expense, allocation or transfer belongs to an Account. This is the wallet ledger relationship.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Account → MoneyLocation</strong><p class="muted">Each wallet Account points to one physical/logical MoneyLocation such as rbsankaran_acc, Amma Cash, Appa Cash, Change Cash or Travel Card.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Owner + MoneyLocation + Account + Allocation → MoneyPool</strong><p class="muted">MoneyPool is the reconciliation layer. It tracks how much money a particular owner has in a particular location and allocation.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Category → SubCategory → Item</strong><p class="muted">This is the expense taxonomy. A Category contains many SubCategories and Items; an Item may optionally belong to a SubCategory.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Transaction → Category / SubCategory / Item</strong><p class="muted">An expense can point into the taxonomy so reports can group spending at category, subcategory or item level. These foreign keys are nullable so uncategorized/custom expenses remain possible.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Item → FoodProfile</strong><p class="muted">A food Item may have one FoodProfile. FoodProfile classifies the item as <b>Food or Drink</b> and stores its food group, health classification and sugary status.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Transaction → FoodEvent → FoodEventItem</strong><p class="muted">Food transactions can have a meal context and one or more food entries. FoodEvent is one-to-one with Transaction; FoodEventItem is many-to-one with FoodEvent.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>Transaction → Transaction</strong><p class="muted">related_tx links the two sides of a wallet transfer so outgoing and incoming ledger rows can be reverted together.</p></div>
-  <div class="category-item"><strong>SavedQuery / QueryExecutionLog</strong><p class="muted">SavedQuery stores reusable SQL. QueryExecutionLog stores read-only playground execution history, status, duration and errors.</p></div>
-</div></section>
